7. Stage 5 — Conflict analysis

This is where I would add your new requirement.

And I would define conflict much more broadly than Git's literal merge conflict.

There are two categories.

A. Mechanical conflict

Example:

PR branch
      +
base branch
      ↓
Git merge
      ↓
CONFLICT

This is straightforward.

RepoMind can detect it using Git/GitHub state.

B. Semantic conflict

This is much more valuable.

A PR may merge cleanly but still conflict with the system.

Example:

Existing code
AUTH_PROVIDER = InternalAuth()
New PR
AUTH_PROVIDER = ExternalAuth()

Git sees no conflict.

But repository architecture says authentication must use the internal provider.

RepoMind detects:

SEMANTIC CONFLICT

Authentication architecture conflict

Changed:
auth/config.py

Repository rule:
Authentication must use InternalAuth.

Existing implementation:
services/auth_service.py

Risk:
High

This is much more interesting than simply running git merge.

8. Types of semantic conflicts RepoMind should detect

I would create these:

Architecture conflict

New implementation violates documented architecture.

API contract conflict

Endpoint behavior conflicts with existing consumers/schema.

Data-model conflict

Database model/migration conflicts with application assumptions.

Security conflict

New behavior conflicts with security policy.

Dependency conflict

New library/version may conflict with existing dependency constraints.

Test contract conflict

Implementation changes behavior without updating affected tests.

Configuration conflict

New configuration contradicts existing deployment/configuration rules.

Recent-change conflict

Another recent PR changed the same critical area.

This gives RepoMind a distinct feature:

Semantic Conflict Detection

That is a strong differentiator.

